{{also|Sterben}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|sterban}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*sterbaną}}, itself either from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*(s)terp-||to lose force; lose sensibility, become numb; be dead, be motionless}} or from {{m|ine-pro|*sterbʰ-||to be stiff, become stiff}}. Cognate with Low German {{m|nds|starven}}, {{m|nds|staarven}}, Dutch {{m|nl|sterven}}, West Frisian {{m|fy|stjerre}}, English {{m|en|starve}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃtɛʁbən/|[ˈʃtɛʁbən]|[ˈʃtɛɐ̯bən]|[ˈʃtɛʁbm̩]|[ˈʃtɛɐ̯bm̩]|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|ster|ben|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-sterben.ogg|audio (Germany)|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-sterben.ogg|audio (Austria)|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=3|stirbt|starb|gestorben|past subjunctive=stürbe|auxiliary=sein}}

# {{label|de|intransitive}} to [[die]]
#: ''Mein Hund ist '''gestorben'''.'' – “My dog has '''died'''.”
# {{label|de|transitive|with accusative or genitive}} to [[die]] of a (particular kind of) [[death]]
#: ''Das Opfer ist einen schrecklichen Tod '''gestorben'''.''
#: ''Das Opfer ist eines schrecklichen Todes '''gestorben'''.''
#:: “The victim '''died''' a terrible death.”

